<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n4640" root="ثلث" body-key="v">
    
      <b><span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">مَثْلَثُ</span></b>  and <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">مَثْلَثَ</span>: see <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">ثُلَاثُ</span>. ― 
       <p class="alpheios-lex-sense-separator"> </p><span class="alpheios-lex-sense-spacer2"> </span>-b2-  <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">مثلث</span> [i. e.
<span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">مَتْلَثٌ</span>] signifies <i>A chord</i> [<i>of a lute</i>] <i>composed of
three twists:</i> that which is of two twists is called
<span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">مثنى</span> [i. e. <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">مَثْنًى</span>]: or, as some say, these two words
signify [respectively] the <i>third chord</i> and the
second: their pls. are <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">مَثَالِثُ</span> and <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">مَثَانٍ</span>. (Har
p.244.)

